  <div class="spec">Edexcel International GCSE Religious Studies (4RS1) · Sources of Wisdom and Authority</div>
  <div class="stage" id="stage">

    <!-- 0 WELCOME -->
    <section class="screen active">
      <span class="tag">Mini-Lesson</span>
      <h1>Sources of Wisdom and Authority</h1>
      <p class="big">Every religion turns to <b>sources of authority</b> to know how to believe, worship and live. Chief among these are the <b>sacred texts (scriptures)</b>, but authority also flows from <b>leaders</b>, <b>traditions</b> and <b>teachings passed down</b>.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A source of authority guides belief, worship and daily life">
          <rect x="18" y="55" width="150" height="60" rx="14" fill="#efe1ff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="93" y="82" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#6a30b8">source of</text>
          <text x="93" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#6a30b8">authority</text>
          <line x1="168" y1="85" x2="392" y2="85" stroke="#34c777" stroke-width="5"/>
          <polygon points="392,85 376,77 376,93" fill="#34c777"/>
          <text x="280" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">guides</text>
          <rect x="396" y="35" width="150" height="34" rx="10" fill="#d9ebff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="471" y="57" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">belief</text>
          <rect x="396" y="72" width="150" height="34" rx="10" fill="#ffe9c7" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="471" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#9a5a00">worship</text>
          <rect x="396" y="109" width="150" height="34" rx="10" fill="#d6f6e4" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="471" y="131" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">daily life</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p>Work through each screen, answer the <b>questions</b> as you go (some are multiple choice, some short writing, some matching games) and collect ⭐ stars. Press <b>Start</b> when you're ready.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 KEY TERMS -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Key vocabulary</span>
      <h2>Words you must know</h2>
      <p>Before we meet each religion's texts, learn the language examiners expect you to use:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Scripture</b> — a sacred, written text with authority for a religion.</li>
        <li><b>Revelation</b> — knowledge or truth believed to be <b>disclosed by God</b> to humans (e.g. through a prophet). Contrasts with knowledge worked out by human reason.</li>
        <li><b>Canon</b> — the fixed, agreed collection of texts a community accepts as authoritative scripture.</li>
        <li><b>Oral tradition</b> — teachings passed on by word of mouth before (or alongside) being written down.</li>
        <li><b>Interpretation</b> — how believers understand a text: reading it <b>literally</b> (word-for-word, exactly as stated) or <b>non-literally</b> (as symbol, metaphor or moral lesson).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Exam tip:</b> "source of authority" is wider than "sacred text". Leaders, worship traditions and long-standing customs also carry authority.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 Q: define revelation (MCQ) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Define a key term</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>In Religious Studies, what does the term <b>revelation</b> most precisely mean?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q1" data-why="Revelation is truth believers hold to be disclosed by God, rather than worked out purely by human reasoning. It is central to how many scriptures claim their authority.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">A public reading of scripture in worship <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Truth believed to be disclosed by God to humans <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The fixed list of accepted holy books <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">A prayer said before reading a text <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 CHRISTIANITY -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Christianity</span>
      <h2>The Bible</h2>
      <p>Christians regard the <span class="hl k">Bible</span> as the sacred word of God, though views on how directly differ. It has two parts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>Old Testament</b> — shared roots with Judaism, including creation, law and the prophets.</li>
        <li>The <b>New Testament</b> — the <b>four Gospels</b> (Matthew, Mark, Luke, John, recording the life and teaching of Jesus), the Acts, letters (epistles) and Revelation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Christians use the Bible for <b>worship</b> (readings and sermons), <b>private study and prayer</b>, and <b>moral guidance</b> (e.g. the teachings of Jesus and the Ten Commandments). The <b>Gospels</b> carry special authority because they record Jesus himself.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Interpretation varies:</b> some Christians read passages such as the creation account <b>literally</b>; others read them <b>non-literally</b>, as conveying deeper spiritual truth.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 ISLAM -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Islam</span>
      <h2>The Qur'an, Hadith and Sunnah</h2>
      <p>For Muslims the <span class="hl c">Qur'an</span> is the supreme authority: believed to be the <b>direct, unaltered word of God (Allah)</b>, revealed in Arabic to the Prophet Muhammad through the angel Jibril (Gabriel).</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Qur'an</b> — the highest source; treated with great reverence and recited in worship (salah) in its original Arabic.</li>
        <li><b>Hadith</b> — recorded reports of what the Prophet Muhammad said, did or approved.</li>
        <li><b>Sunnah</b> — the example, customs and practice of the Prophet, drawn largely from the Hadith, used as a model for living.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Muslims use these to guide belief, the <b>Five Pillars</b>, law and everyday conduct. Where the Qur'an is not explicit, believers look to the Sunnah and Hadith.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Common misconception:</b> the Qur'an is <b>not</b> seen as one prophet's ideas — Muslims hold it to be the <b>literal, direct word of God</b>, unchanged since revelation.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 Q: match text to religion (match game 1) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Match it</span>
      <h2>Text ↔ religion</h2>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Tap a sacred text on the left, then tap the religion it belongs to on the right.</p>
      <div class="activity">
        <div id="matchGame"></div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-match"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 JUDAISM -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Judaism</span>
      <h2>The Tenakh (Torah) and Talmud</h2>
      <p>The Jewish scriptures are the <span class="hl g">Tenakh</span>, made of three parts — the <b>Torah</b> (Law), <b>Nevi'im</b> (Prophets) and <b>Ketuvim</b> (Writings).</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <b>Torah</b> (the first five books) is the most sacred, believed to have been given by God to Moses. Its scroll (Sefer Torah) is read in the synagogue with great honour.</li>
        <li>The <b>Talmud</b> is a vast record of rabbis' teachings, discussion and interpretation of the law — it explains how the Torah is applied to daily life.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Jews use the Torah for <b>worship, festivals and the 613 mitzvot (commandments)</b> that shape daily conduct. Written Torah and the <b>oral tradition</b> (later written as the Talmud) work together.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 Q: how a source is used (short answer) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Short answer</span>
      <h2>Explain how a source is used</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">1</span><span>In one or two sentences, explain <b>one</b> way Muslims use the <b>Sunnah</b> as a source of authority.</span></div>
        <div class="textans" data-t="t1">
          <textarea placeholder="Write your answer here…" aria-label="Your answer"></textarea>
          <button class="checkbtn">Reveal model answer</button>
        </div>
        <div class="model" id="model-t1">
          <div class="mt">Model answer</div>
          The Sunnah is the example and practice of the Prophet Muhammad. Muslims follow it as a model for how to live — for instance, using it to know <b>how</b> to perform actions such as prayer, or to guide conduct where the Qur'an does not give explicit detail.
        </div>
        <div class="hint">Aim to name what the Sunnah is <b>and</b> give one clear use.</div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-t1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 HINDUISM -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Hinduism</span>
      <h2>The Vedas, Upanishads and Bhagavad Gita</h2>
      <p>Hindu scriptures are traditionally divided into two types:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Shruti ("that which is heard")</b> — the oldest and most authoritative, held to be eternal truth received by ancient sages. This includes the <span class="hl y">Vedas</span> and the <b>Upanishads</b> (philosophical teaching on Brahman and the self).</li>
        <li><b>Smriti ("that which is remembered")</b> — texts of human authorship, including the great epics. The <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>, part of the Mahabharata, is a much-loved and widely used teaching on duty (dharma) and devotion.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Vedas began as an <b>oral tradition</b>, carefully memorised and recited long before being written. Hindus draw on these texts for <b>worship (puja), festivals and guidance on dharma</b>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 Q: shruti vs smriti (MCQ) -->
