    <!-- 10 BUDDHISM -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Buddhism</span>
      <h2>The Tipitaka (Pali Canon)</h2>
      <p>Buddhism has no single creator-God revealing a text; authority rests above all on the <b>teachings of the Buddha (the Dhamma)</b>. The most important early scripture is the <span class="hl t">Tipitaka</span>, meaning "three baskets":</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Vinaya Pitaka</b> — rules for the monastic community (the Sangha).</li>
        <li><b>Sutta Pitaka</b> — discourses and teachings of the Buddha.</li>
        <li><b>Abhidhamma Pitaka</b> — deeper philosophical and psychological analysis.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Buddha's teachings were preserved by <b>oral tradition</b> and recitation for centuries before being written down in Pali. Buddhists also honour the <b>Sangha</b> (community and teachers) as a source of guidance. Different traditions (Theravada, Mahayana) value additional texts.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 SIKHISM -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Sikhism</span>
      <h2>The Guru Granth Sahib</h2>
      <p>For Sikhs the <span class="hl el">Guru Granth Sahib</span> is the supreme scripture. It contains hymns of the Sikh Gurus and other holy writers, compiled and finalised as the scripture of the faith.</p>
      <p>Uniquely, Guru Gobind Singh declared that after the ten human Gurus there would be <b>no further living Guru</b> — the scripture itself would be the <b>eternal Guru</b>. So Sikhs treat the Guru Granth Sahib as a <b>living Guru</b>: it is placed on a throne (takht), given a canopy, and treated with the honour due to a Guru.</p>
      <p>It is central to <b>worship in the gurdwara</b>, to naming ceremonies and weddings, and to daily guidance.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Common misconception:</b> the Guru Granth Sahib is not treated as "just a book" — for Sikhs it holds the status and authority of a <b>living Guru</b>.</p>
    </section>

