      <h2>Authority beyond the page</h2>
      <p>Texts are not the only authority. Religions also look to <b>leaders, teachers and long-standing tradition</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Christianity</b> — the Pope (for Roman Catholics), bishops, ministers and the teaching tradition of the Church help interpret the Bible.</li>
        <li><b>Islam</b> — imams and scholars help apply the Qur'an and Sunnah; consensus of scholars can guide the community.</li>
        <li><b>Judaism</b> — rabbis teach and interpret the law; the Talmud records this tradition of interpretation.</li>
        <li><b>Hinduism &amp; Buddhism</b> — a guru or teacher, and the community (Sangha), pass on and explain the teachings.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Why it matters:</b> when a text is unclear or does not mention a modern issue, believers often turn to <b>leaders and tradition</b> to decide how to act.</p>
